the voyage we're going to talk about  today began as a secret president Thomas  Jefferson had a plan one that he'd been  nurturing for years now the United  States was less than a generation old  and most of its people still lived  clustered in the original colonies along  the Atlantic coast in fact in the early  19th century an estimated two-thirds of  all citizens lived within 50 miles of  the Atlantic Ocean  yet Jefferson was convinced that the  destiny of the United States was to be  far larger continental in scale in other  words Jefferson had his eyes on the  great expanses of the West's places that  were then claimed by France Spain  Britain even Russia given that these  regions had not been mapped what  surprises might they still contain an  abiding fantasy that we've already seen  capturing the minds of many explorers  was the idea of a Northwest Passage a  great waterway cutting across the top of  North America giving access to Asia back  in 1611 Henry Hudson had died on a quest  for that voyage for that passage and no  one since had located it but perhaps  something like that might exist if not a  great waterway on a more modest scale  there might still be larger navigable  rivers that formed a network across the  North American interior that could be  exploited a network that would unite the  Atlantic with the Pacific and thus save  the long trip around the tip of South  America that Magellan had pioneered  centuries before so the prize might be  great the very fact that a Scotsman  named Alexander Mackenzie had already  crossed Canada from ocean to ocean in  1793 just made the matter even more  urgent Geoffrey Jefferson was convinced  that now was the time to find out by  whatever means necessary  so in mid-january 1803 Jefferson sent a  secret proposal to Congress Jefferson  urged that an expedition be funded by  the US government to travel beyond the  Mississippi River  beyond the boundaries of the United  States toward the Rocky Mountains to  chart and explore these areas most of  them claimed by France an American  officer and troops were to be sent out  to journey along the Missouri River to  get to know the local Native American  tribes and especially to try to locate  the Northwest Passage if it existed for  this undertaking Jefferson requested  that Congress authorize the vast sum of  $2,500 in spite of this huge expense  Congress agreed Jefferson urged that the  expedition be officially called a  scientific and literary undertaking so  as to mask its true geopolitical and  strategic importance and Jefferson  already knew who the leader should be of  that expedition a man he had chosen for  this task earlier and had actually  appointed as his own personal secretary  Meriweather Lewis the 29 year old Lewis  was like Jefferson a native of Virginia  he also had been an officer in clashes  with Native American tribes on the Ohio  frontier which was then the Wild West  Lewis was intelligent resourceful and  resilient so Jefferson believed that he  was the ideal man for the task sensing  to the challenge would be so large in  this undertaking that he would need help  Lewis also wrote to an old friend asking  if he would partner in leading the  expedition this was William Clark  another Virginia born Army officer who  had frontier experience in fighting the  Native Americans Clark was an  extroverted cheerful man with bright red  hair he was older than Lewis and in fact  had been his superior in the army but he  was now retired  Clark readily agreed and this proved to  be a most successful partnership once  the expedition began Lewis was formally  in charge but they worked as equals  something about their temperaments  complemented each other and they agreed  this human connection proved vital to  the success of the journey in April 1805  the Corps was ready at last to leave  Fort Mandan and resumed their journey  west in smaller boats for the leaders  this was an epic moment up until this  point they had traversed areas that were  known that were familiar to trappers and  at Fort Mandan they had been at the far  edge of the familiar from now on from  this point they were literally going off  the map into areas that were simply  unknown to them we've noted a theme in  our course of one set of journeys  prompting other journeys and here we can  actually see that inspiration very  clearly because Lewis as he sort of  savored and thought about this moment of  stepping off the map wrote in his  journal his thoughts this is what he  said  this little fleet although not quite so  respectable as those of Columbus or  Captain Cook were still viewed by us  with as much pleasure as those  deservedly famed adventurers ever beheld  theirs we were now about to penetrate a  country on which the foot of civilized  man had never trodden the good or evil  it had in store for us was for  experiment yet to determine
 

